<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Balaton</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Balaton is a freshwater lake in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, about 77 kilometres long and almost 600 square kilometres in area. It lay in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and, in the 9th century, in the Frankish-ruled principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocel">Kocel</a>, centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalavar">Zalavár</a> to its south-west. Its shores became Hungary's main holiday region in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-balaton.jpg" alt="Lake Balaton" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: txd · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balaton_Hungary_Landscape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
